00:02:51.880 --> 00:03:00.000
I was born in New York City in 1936 in October and I spent the first five years

00:03:00.060 --> 00:03:03.220
my life started going back and forth between New York and California, because my parents

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:11.270
were divorced, my mother was in California, and my father was in New York. My mother was

00:03:11.270 --> 00:03:19.370
a singer and a lyricist. She wrote, "Love is a symbol, bing, da, da, da, da." My father

00:03:19.370 --> 00:03:23.250
was an attorney and had nothing to do with the arts, particularly, but I was raised with

00:03:23.250 --> 00:03:28.500
him from the age of five on. At the age of 14, I began studying percussion. At the age

00:03:28.500 --> 00:03:30.000
of 16, I went to Cornell University.

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.190
where I ended up being a philosophy major and a drummer on the weekends.

00:03:35.190 --> 00:03:39.670
My music studies there were especially with Professor William Austin, whose encouragement,

00:03:39.670 --> 00:03:43.310
I think, eventually steered me into becoming a composer.

00:03:43.310 --> 00:03:49.850
I left Cornell at the age of 19 and went down to New York City and began studying privately

00:03:49.850 --> 00:03:53.400
with Hall Overton, who was a composer and also a jazz musician, a good friend of Thelonious

00:03:53.400 --> 00:03:54.400
Monks.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:04:00.000
After about two years with Hall, I entered the Juilliard School here in New York City.

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:08.460
Before that time, I was a cab driver, worked in the U.S. post office, and it wasn't until

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:12.100
I would say, play another one, play another one, this period around 1970 when I began

00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:16.440
to say to myself, where in the world is drumming the main voice of the orchestra?

00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:18.480
Well, there's two answers, African and Indonesian.

00:04:19.420 --> 00:04:23.940
You see, there was a period where I really made no bones about being a drummer, but starting

00:04:23.940 --> 00:04:25.600
in Juilliard, I kind of swept that under the rug.

00:04:25.600 --> 00:04:29.700
After all, classical composers aren't bebop drummers.

00:04:30.600 --> 00:04:39.770
I think what I liked about the music so much was the joy of the music.

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:44.950
I felt there was a great, exultant, joyous quality in the pieces.

00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:53.220
And then when I first played them, I discovered that it was so much fun to play them and that

00:04:53.220 --> 00:05:00.000
even though the numbers of notes were in theory limited, that it caused one to think about

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:04.830
notes in all kinds of different ways and so the experience of being in the midst

00:05:04.830 --> 00:05:14.560
of the music was quite wonderful.

00:06:24.730 --> 00:06:29.960
counterpoint that I heard was a version that Steve had put together

00:06:30.030 --> 00:06:34.210
on his little electronic piano or something

00:06:34.210 --> 00:06:38.660
of as many of the parts as he could get together on tape.

00:06:38.660 --> 00:06:42.260
And I put that on my cassette recorder at home

00:06:42.260 --> 00:06:43.600
and played the beginning of it.

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:47.880
And I never forget, my kids came running down from upstairs.

00:06:47.880 --> 00:06:50.380
They said, "Daddy, daddy, daddy, turn off the record player.

00:06:50.380 --> 00:06:50.900
It's broken.

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:51.680
It keeps breaking.

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:52.680
Something's wrong."

00:06:52.680 --> 00:06:54.300
And I said, "No, I'm afraid this is something

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:56.710
daddy's going to play."

00:06:56.710 --> 00:07:00.000
But anyway, later on, I put it together.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:03.960
with all the clarinet parts and added sort of you know the human element

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:10.430
instead of just the mechanical electronic sounds and I liked it I

00:07:10.430 --> 00:07:14.390
gradually got more and more to like it especially when I had a chance to give

00:07:14.390 --> 00:07:18.470
it my own inflection and finally when the finished version came out the kids

00:07:18.470 --> 00:07:21.700
were we're down there in the living room dancing to it

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:12.960
The piece Four Organs took place, and basically it's about augmentation.

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:15.420
Short chord gets long, very long indeed.

00:08:15.420 --> 00:08:20.300
And a duration of 20 minutes is probably one of the most abrasive pieces I ever wrote.

00:08:35.600 --> 00:08:42.940
we did this in a program of the Boston Symphony Orchestra and we got about I'd

00:08:42.940 --> 00:08:48.690
say a third of the way into the piece when protests began to erupt the nature

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:51.930
of these outbursts got more and more violent one woman came running down the

00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:56.070
aisle and stamped on the front of the stage with her fists and said I can't

00:08:56.070 --> 00:09:00.000
stand it I can't stand it the audience became wilder and

00:09:00.000 --> 00:09:02.960
a while that the ushers were moving about not knowing whether they should call the police

00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:06.520
because certain people were getting a bit violent and little by little the noise became

00:09:06.520 --> 00:09:11.740
so great that even on stage with these amplified instruments we could not hear one another

00:09:11.740 --> 00:09:15.460
and we were trying to get through the cycle the complicated rhythmic cycle of this piece

00:09:15.460 --> 00:09:19.880
the only way we could do it was that we were looking at one another and i was going steve

00:09:19.880 --> 00:09:30.000
together you were going like mouthing numbers and i immediately had this flashback to stravinsky's

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:35.320
of the premiere of Sakura, where Nijinsky was backstage yelling out numbers to the dancers

00:09:35.320 --> 00:09:38.180
so they would know where they were supposed to dance, because they couldn't hear the orchestra

00:09:38.180 --> 00:09:38.640
anymore.

00:09:39.040 --> 00:09:41.060
I had this total identification with that moment.

00:09:41.490 --> 00:09:43.250
We finally reached the end of the piece.

00:09:43.250 --> 00:09:51.720
There was one moment of silence, and then there was an avalanche of bravos, boos, whistles,

00:09:52.360 --> 00:09:54.760
standing ovations, violent threats.

00:09:54.880 --> 00:09:56.320
It was really quite something.

00:09:56.920 --> 00:09:59.030
Steve, I know, was really stunned.

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:00.000
I...

00:10:00.350 --> 00:10:04.810
Definitely, you know taken aback by the whole experience and I was so happy. I said Steve. This is it

00:10:05.090 --> 00:10:08.450
This is fantastic. You realize what you've done. You've gotten under their skin

00:10:28.400 --> 00:10:30.000
Come out is the second

00:10:30.410 --> 00:10:36.840
piece that I would choose to keep of my own. It was composed in 1966, and it's a tape piece.

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:40.940
It uses the voice of a black kid who was arrested for murder.

00:10:40.940 --> 00:10:50.110
It was done as a part of a benefit for the retrial of a group of six black kids who had

00:10:50.110 --> 00:10:56.180
been arrested for the murder of a white woman in the Bronx in 1964, I believe it happened,

00:10:56.180 --> 00:11:00.000
and they were referred to as the Harlem Six.

00:11:00.960 --> 00:11:03.610
And I was approached by a man by the name of Truman Nelson,

00:11:03.610 --> 00:11:06.010
who said he understood that I did something with tape,

00:11:06.010 --> 00:11:10.110
and would I be willing to edit this pile of tapes he had

00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:13.550
of boys, police, mothers, and so on and so forth for a benefit.

00:11:13.550 --> 00:11:15.870
I said, well, you know, it's not really something I do,

00:11:15.870 --> 00:11:19.270
but I'll consider it if you will allow me to make a piece

00:11:19.270 --> 00:11:22.370
of anything that strikes my ear.

00:11:22.370 --> 00:11:25.370
And out of this stack of audio tape, lasting maybe 10 hours,

00:11:25.370 --> 00:11:27.270
I heard this one phrase come out to show them,

00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:30.000
dee-dum, ba-da-dum, which just sort of grabbed my ear and said,

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:36.210
that's the one um you could say in a sense that that was my vocal music at that period of time

00:11:36.210 --> 00:11:39.810
and that kind of thinking very much came out of the kind of thinking let's say that the american

00:11:39.810 --> 00:11:46.020
poet william carlos williams suggested he said my poetry comes out of american speech rhythms as

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:52.100
they're found on the streets uh and as a student when i was at juilliard trying to set williams's

00:11:52.100 --> 00:12:00.000
poetry i found that i was setting them and killing them by setting into a musical fixed meter uh so the tape pieces

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:06.010
for me, both It's Gonna Rain and Come Out, were like taking Williams's advice, finding

00:12:06.010 --> 00:12:10.580
speech exactly as it's spoken, photographically if you will, taped, and then intensifying

00:12:10.580 --> 00:12:15.000
that by repetition. I had to like open the bruise up and let

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:30.000
I'm gonna let some of the blue blood come out to show them.

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:37.300
I was interested in the manipulation of human speech because it's loaded with acoustic richness and meaning.

00:12:37.300 --> 00:13:00.000
Come out has a directly political overtone to it, but I would say this, that I

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:01.930
I always like to think of people

00:13:01.930 --> 00:13:03.310
like Kurt Weill and Hans Eisler.

00:13:04.040 --> 00:13:06.240
They both were good Germans in a bad time.

00:13:06.420 --> 00:13:08.240
They both were perhaps good socialists

00:13:08.240 --> 00:13:09.580
at a fascist time.

00:13:10.380 --> 00:13:11.920
And as a Jew in Germany at the time,

00:13:11.980 --> 00:13:13.500
I would have been glad to have both of them on my side

00:13:13.500 --> 00:13:14.500
in helping me to live.

00:13:15.320 --> 00:13:19.740
But life is cruel and man uncouth.

00:13:20.200 --> 00:13:22.760
Alas, Eisler's music, to me,

00:13:22.820 --> 00:13:25.220
comes across as heavy-handed, dogged,

00:13:25.220 --> 00:13:27.690
and we have to give him an E for effort,

00:13:28.310 --> 00:13:29.890
but his music is a bore.

00:13:30.350 --> 00:13:31.180
I believe.

00:13:31.180 --> 00:13:35.320
Kurt Weill is a musical genius, he has a lightness, he's an irony, and the music continues

00:13:35.320 --> 00:13:39.280
to live, whatever its political content.

00:13:39.280 --> 00:13:45.730
I believe Wagner was an anti-Semite, Wagner was a proto-Nazi, Wagner is the kind of man

00:13:45.730 --> 00:13:49.980
I would have been happy to draw in my sights with a rifle and blow his brains out, but

00:13:49.980 --> 00:13:54.960
Wagner is also a great composer, and you've just got to lump it.

00:13:54.960 --> 00:13:55.990
That's the truth.

00:14:04.600 --> 00:14:07.960
Country music really evolved out of my contact with visual artists.

00:14:07.960 --> 00:14:13.180
In particular, I was doing a theatrical event, which is what they were called in those days.

00:14:13.180 --> 00:14:17.770
We were doing it, I remember standing in this room in the art department, University of Colorado,

00:14:17.770 --> 00:14:22.510
with a couple of microphones literally on my hand as if they were yo-yos or keychains,

00:14:22.510 --> 00:14:25.200
and just sort of idly swinging them.

00:14:25.200 --> 00:14:29.030
As it would go by the tape recorder, it would go, "Yeeep!" "Yeeep!"

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:34.020
was struck by that because what it was is that it was feedback as the microphone zipped by the

00:14:34.020 --> 00:14:38.680
speaker but feedback is usually assumed that it's howling constant sound and I suddenly realized

00:14:38.680 --> 00:15:00.000
what was happening is that I was making rhythmic feedback. What happened was that in this period of time from let's say

00:15:00.000 --> 00:15:05.520
1965 up to 1970. There was a kind of a back-and-forth in my life between the

00:15:05.520 --> 00:15:10.880
great interest in tape, therefore electronics if you will, and live music. I

00:15:10.880 --> 00:15:15.680
felt on the one hand that there were very, very interesting ideas that I had

00:15:15.680 --> 00:15:19.400
derived in using tape loops in particular. We talked about come out and

00:15:19.400 --> 00:15:24.070
it's gonna rain. On the other hand, I felt without any question that if these

00:15:24.070 --> 00:15:30.000
ideas applied only to tape, then indeed they were gimmicks and had the future of a gimmick.

00:15:30.670 --> 00:15:36.210
And the only thing that would prove that they weren't gimmicks was that they would be transferable to live musicians.

00:15:36.450 --> 00:15:39.720
The first, and I would say probably the most important,

00:15:40.660 --> 00:15:47.300
bridging that happened was in a piece called Piano Face, where in 1967, in really desperation,

00:15:47.720 --> 00:15:51.040
the desperation of, let us say, a mad scientist trapped in the laboratory,

00:15:52.030 --> 00:15:56.470
I felt this tape thing can't be done by people.

00:15:56.470 --> 00:16:00.000
Somebody can't get in unison with another repeating pattern and gradually,

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:01.680
increase the speed to at least one beat ahead of it.

00:16:01.680 --> 00:16:03.960
That's indigenous to tape recorders.

00:16:03.960 --> 00:16:05.400
People can't do that.

00:16:05.400 --> 00:16:08.320
But on the other hand, I thought, this is a fantastic process.

00:16:08.320 --> 00:16:11.580
This is a very interesting way to make music.

00:16:11.580 --> 00:16:14.900
So in desperation, I recorded a little repeating pattern

00:16:14.900 --> 00:16:17.580
at the piano, made a tape loop of it, put on tape recorder,

00:16:17.580 --> 00:16:19.320
and began playing against it.

00:16:19.320 --> 00:16:20.680
I said, OK, I'm a second tape recorder.

00:16:20.680 --> 00:16:21.890
Let's go.

00:16:21.890 --> 00:16:23.810
And I found, to my surprise, I could do it.

00:16:23.810 --> 00:16:27.900
I could, if I listened quite carefully and moved very slowly,

00:16:27.900 --> 00:16:30.000
I could stay in unison with myself and then

00:16:31.050 --> 00:17:00.000
increased my speed until I was 1/16th note ahead at 70. It seemed after drumming on the keyboard that one should perhaps go back to drumming on the drum. So I made arrangements to go to Ghana and actually

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:05.110
ended up studying the music of the Abe tribe, which was precisely the tribe, which was analyzed

00:17:05.110 --> 00:17:09.800
in a book called "Studies in African Music" by the Englishman A.M. Jones, and whose book

00:17:09.800 --> 00:17:14.500
was the first book that I had discovered on African music with scores. So in a sense,

00:17:14.500 --> 00:17:21.310
when I went to Africa, I recapitulated in real life what I had learned from a book.

00:17:21.310 --> 00:17:28.010
I don't think that while being in Africa I learned anything really new, but what I did

00:17:28.010 --> 00:17:30.000
get that was perhaps more valuable was the Great

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:34.730
pat on the back. And the pat on the back fits something like this. Yes, acoustic instruments

00:17:34.730 --> 00:17:40.050
are richer and more complex than electronic ones. Yes, percussion can be the dominant

00:17:40.050 --> 00:17:50.100
voice in very complex classical music. And yes, that music can swing. Go home and do it.

00:18:00.250 --> 00:18:08.130
Thank you.

00:18:30.000 --> 00:18:36.470
think oh a young american goes to africa comes home and writes drum piece that is completely

00:18:36.470 --> 00:18:41.200
mistaken i was a drummer when i was 14. what the trip to africa did to say was you were drummer when

00:18:41.200 --> 00:18:43.800
you're 14 now be yourself

00:19:00.160 --> 00:19:18.850
So drumming happened when I came back, and the phenomena of writing drumming for my ensemble did a number of things, one of which was to change the numbers of players in my ensemble from about 5 to 12, and it also began our travels to Europe.

00:19:37.740 --> 00:19:43.460
Drumming is the longest work I've ever composed. Drumming has a variability in its length because

00:19:43.460 --> 00:19:47.350
of the number of repeats. It's free and left to the players within certain limits. And

00:19:47.350 --> 00:19:50.330
I would say that it would range from about an hour and five minutes to an hour and twenty

00:19:50.330 --> 00:19:52.760
minutes in performance.

00:20:03.610 --> 00:20:15.770
It doesn't just have drums. There are many people who think that it has just drums because we have over the years an ensemble and now other ensembles have begun playing drumming part one, which is merely for the tune drums stand mounted and played with sticks.

00:20:15.770 --> 00:20:30.000
Drumming part two, which goes without any pause, is for three marimbas played by nine players and with two women's voices.

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:35.730
drumming part three which follows immediately without any poises for three glockenspiels whistling and piccolo

00:22:01.470 --> 00:22:04.470
Tapping music began in a nightclub in Brussels.

00:22:05.530 --> 00:22:08.550
We were playing a concert in Brussels, I think it was 1971.

00:22:08.550 --> 00:22:12.060
We were touring drumming or parts of drumming.

00:22:12.060 --> 00:22:21.030
And besides our bodies, we were toting around 2,000 pounds of crates filled with marimbas, drums, blockenspiels, amplifiers, loudspeakers.

00:22:21.030 --> 00:22:25.180
And a separate truck had to accompany us as we went.

00:22:25.180 --> 00:22:30.000
And after this concert in Brussels, we went out to a very sort of ratty flamenco club.

00:22:31.180 --> 00:22:35.420
And there were a couple of women there playing guitar and singing, and they played guitar

00:22:35.420 --> 00:22:36.140
quite terribly.

00:22:36.320 --> 00:22:39.880
I remember they had lipsticks that were up to their noses, and they couldn't really sing.

00:22:39.940 --> 00:22:44.010
But when they clapped, it was ear-splitting and just incredibly effective.

00:22:44.010 --> 00:22:50.810
So we left out into the foggy Belgian evening, fooling around with clapping patterns, one

00:22:50.810 --> 00:22:51.490
against the other.

00:22:51.890 --> 00:22:54.010
And when I got home after that trip, I thought a couple of things.

00:22:54.090 --> 00:22:55.770
Number one, it would be fun to do it.

00:22:55.830 --> 00:23:00.000
Number two, I wanted something that we could do without that 2,000 pounds of a clap.

00:23:00.000 --> 00:23:03.220
equipment, something that could be done anywhere, anytime.

00:23:03.220 --> 00:23:07.300
All we had to do was be there, we would bring the instruments with us.

00:23:39.400 --> 00:23:47.040
So it would seem much more appropriate in that piece to simply go from one rhythmic relationship to the next on the spot with no further ado and no gradual sliding.

00:23:47.320 --> 00:23:55.710
It was a small change, but it was a significant one because every piece from 1965 to 1971 had involved this gradual shift.

00:24:02.140 --> 00:24:05.100
Music for pieces of wood was a kind of extension of that.

00:24:05.100 --> 00:24:10.460
One thing I had remembered when I was in Africa is that very often at dances,

00:24:10.460 --> 00:24:13.870
informal dances on a weekend evening in Ghana,

00:24:13.870 --> 00:24:16.840
people would pick up pieces of wood, literally, that had been sawed off.

00:24:16.840 --> 00:24:20.890
They looked like, you know, excess, sort of spare pieces,

00:24:20.890 --> 00:24:27.270
and very loudly clap them together, basically as a physical way of amplifying their clapping.

00:24:27.270 --> 00:24:30.000
So with those things in mind, and with the success I felt,

00:24:30.000 --> 00:24:33.720
music in mind I thought I would kind of extend that a bit and do a piece where we

00:24:33.720 --> 00:24:38.580
could carry a little canvas sack with us with the entire orchestration with a

00:24:38.580 --> 00:24:43.230
five-man piece and it involved different technique one that appeared in drumming

00:24:43.230 --> 00:24:46.410
and became very important later on in many pieces which is building up a

00:24:46.410 --> 00:24:54.140
pattern from nothing to something if you do that when somebody else is already

00:24:54.140 --> 00:24:57.090
playing that pattern you will end up playing it against them

00:24:57.090 --> 00:25:00.000
so many beats out of phase and create a little cannon around

00:25:00.640 --> 00:25:05.480
And Music for Pieces of Wood is the first piece to really explore that in depth.

00:25:52.970 --> 00:26:00.000
In the early days, and this would be almost all of my music up to 1980, we were playing chamber music.

00:26:00.000 --> 00:26:00.860
the

00:26:00.860 --> 00:26:04.360
lifeblood of the music was its rhythmic ambiguity. And by rhythmic

00:26:04.360 --> 00:26:08.140
ambiguity, I mean particularly that the sense of where is the one,

00:26:08.140 --> 00:26:11.080
which is usually crystal clear in Western music, the most gross example being

00:26:11.080 --> 00:26:13.800
mmm-pah-pah, mmm-pah-pah,

00:26:13.800 --> 00:26:18.200
became in my music very unclear, very difficult to find. And in rehearsal,

00:26:18.200 --> 00:26:23.590
our problem as players was to remember where one was, because it's very hard to

00:26:23.590 --> 00:26:25.390
play unless you realize that.

00:26:25.390 --> 00:26:30.000
But if you're playing strongly against it, and if in one sense the answer

00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:34.760
to where is the downbeat is, well, Russell has it here, Bob has it here, and Steve has

00:26:34.760 --> 00:26:41.300
it there, then you create a situation of rhythmic ambiguity where, in one sense of analysis,

00:26:41.300 --> 00:26:51.510
there are three equal points that receive equal stress as possible beginnings.

00:26:51.510 --> 00:26:55.460
This is also extremely important.

00:26:55.460 --> 00:27:00.000
If you're going to write repetitious music, and if the music reviewers are going to say,

00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:05.300
monotonous and for them to be false and not true then one must build something

00:27:05.300 --> 00:27:09.260
into the music which in fact makes it false and untrue and what that is in my

