00:30:27.210 --> 00:30:28.070
three days.

00:31:30.000 --> 00:32:00.000
I began writing music for 18 musicians in 1974, quite a bit in 1977 and 1978, I read that we played it so much that I almost had to

00:32:00.000 --> 00:32:04.000
how much touring I was doing because I wasn't getting much writing there and so

00:32:04.000 --> 00:32:09.160
we got together again we turns out to be I would say about the 12 or 13 out of

00:32:09.160 --> 00:32:17.880
18 of the original people are still very much involved so the continuity with

00:32:17.880 --> 00:32:20.260
the players is enormous

00:33:10.430 --> 00:33:18.020
of course. It was the luscious and most colorful piece I had ever attempted to do. It was also

00:33:18.020 --> 00:33:23.680
harmonically the most involved. There were more changes in the harmony in the first two or three

00:33:23.680 --> 00:33:29.150
minutes of Music for 18 Musicians than there had been in previous pieces laid end to end.

00:33:32.780 --> 00:33:38.280
How do you feel when other people play your music who haven't got any of your ensemble included in their orchestra?

00:33:38.280 --> 00:33:41.800
Well, again, it depends wildly. I mean, I'll give you two extreme cases.

00:33:41.800 --> 00:33:48.110
One case was the Cologne premiere of the desert music was really very poor,

00:33:48.110 --> 00:33:51.540
partly because there were some problems with the score and the seating of the orchestra,

00:33:51.540 --> 00:33:54.860
which were changed in New York, but largely because the orchestra was somewhat older.

00:33:54.860 --> 00:33:59.600
The German and the chorus was made up of basically Hausfrau types.

00:34:00.030 --> 00:34:06.180
And to hear "It is a principal music to repeat the theme" is just not very inspiring.

00:34:06.180 --> 00:34:11.720
And yet, it was clear to me at the time that I couldn't go up to them and say "No, look, no vibrato."

00:34:11.720 --> 00:34:14.720
And it's done much more "marcado."

00:34:14.720 --> 00:34:18.840
Because I could see that in their bodies, in their beings, they simply were not the desert music.

00:34:18.840 --> 00:34:20.400
And my words would simply be a waste of time.

00:34:20.400 --> 00:34:24.960
So I sort of sat back and said, "Well, this is a disaster and that's the way it's going to be."

00:34:24.960 --> 00:34:30.000
On the other hand, switch to San Francisco, I think it was 1982,

00:34:30.220 --> 00:34:37.640
John Adams, the composer and conductor, was conducting a program of my music out there using only members of the San Francisco Symphony.

00:34:38.200 --> 00:34:44.480
And one of the pieces they were doing was music for mallet instruments, voices and organ, which is a piece that's been done 95% with my own ensemble.

00:34:44.970 --> 00:34:50.250
But I do have two sets of parts that I send out, one that we do and one that other people do.

00:34:50.250 --> 00:34:53.430
the difference one of the major differences is that when we do it to save

00:34:53.430 --> 00:35:00.000
money and traveling with too much equipment is we have four marimba players stand facing

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:05.120
each other on two marimbas so that one person is standing wrong he's in back of the instrument

00:35:05.120 --> 00:35:09.300
but when i got to the rehearsal i noticed that the two principal percussionists of the san

00:35:09.300 --> 00:35:13.540
francisco symphony who i didn't know and who were rather young were facing each other on one

00:35:13.540 --> 00:35:19.830
instrument and i uh played dumb and i went up i said uh what are you doing that for and he said

00:35:19.830 --> 00:35:25.600
oh we know you guys do it and it's a lot more fun that way and i sat back and heard them play and

00:35:25.600 --> 00:35:30.000
of course they played the piece very idiomatically very correctly and with a great deal of

00:35:30.000 --> 00:35:36.440
good feeling. I think that that kind of movement that I just described is

00:35:36.440 --> 00:35:41.140
increasing as time passes and will even affect, let's say, younger German

00:35:41.140 --> 00:35:45.160
musicians and not just here in America. But national differences count. And if

00:35:45.160 --> 00:35:48.540
you don't believe it, I mean, just think about von Karajan conducting Gershwin.

00:35:48.540 --> 00:35:50.420
Would you really want to hear that?

00:36:22.620 --> 00:36:30.000
in in the section head i was writing a percussion piece and it was now 10 years uh it was 15 years since

00:36:30.000 --> 00:36:34.340
drumming and about 10 years since Music for 18 Musicians, and it was very important for

00:36:34.340 --> 00:36:39.590
me to do something with percussion that I hadn't done before, to get a different sound

00:36:39.590 --> 00:36:45.430
world out of it. For me, the bowing of the vibraphone and sextet is one of the basic

00:36:45.430 --> 00:36:48.560
timbres in the piece. It fills the second movement.

00:37:08.890 --> 00:37:13.020
What sort of room is there for personal expression in the music?

00:37:13.020 --> 00:37:19.390
I mean, do you feel sort of, well how do I put it, chained to the notes on the page in

00:37:19.390 --> 00:37:20.500
any way?

00:37:20.500 --> 00:37:21.580
Okay.

00:37:21.580 --> 00:37:28.160
Well, as much as we are in Western classical music chained to any part, people can play

00:37:28.160 --> 00:37:30.000
the same Beethoven sonata.

00:37:30.000 --> 00:37:35.200
same notes and of course it comes out very much differently there are there are the same kinds of

00:37:35.200 --> 00:37:40.100
inflections in a performance of a steve reich piece though it may not seem that apparent on

00:37:40.100 --> 00:37:49.840
the surface and it depends on one's exposure to these when i first started playing this kind of

00:37:49.840 --> 00:37:54.530
music it was difficult i would feel as though i was going to forget what i was doing it's a very

00:37:54.530 --> 00:38:00.000
funny thing that happens your hands go on moving and your mind seems to space out

00:38:01.280 --> 00:38:07.690
But after, I would say, the first dozen rehearsals, I began to find that I could concentrate much, much better.

00:38:07.690 --> 00:38:13.140
But in the case of Steve's music, especially the early music, where there isn't much reading that you have to do,

00:38:13.280 --> 00:38:18.890
and the changes happen very gradually, you can be concentrating on what you're hearing around you,

00:38:19.110 --> 00:38:20.750
as well as on what you yourself are doing.

00:38:21.250 --> 00:38:30.000
For myself, I find the most involving...

00:38:30.440 --> 00:38:37.140
aspect of this music is a physical one, more than intellectual and probably more than spiritual.

00:38:37.140 --> 00:38:45.090
I feel like it's incredibly exhilarating to play over a long period of time, to play something

00:38:45.090 --> 00:38:51.380
that feels good to do and involves your body to the extent that this music does for a percussionist.

00:38:51.380 --> 00:38:57.370
And for us, it's great for a change to be playing all the time, and I especially like

00:38:57.370 --> 00:39:00.000
it when we're playing some of the orchestra pieces.

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:03.000
String players have to count about 100 bars and we can play straight through.

00:44:41.200 --> 00:44:49.940
