00:45:19.860 --> 00:45:22.460
in being able to do what it is that i do

00:45:22.460 --> 00:45:29.510
and finding that in fact there are many people out there who it means something to as well

00:45:30.190 --> 00:45:34.370
Ransom Wilson, the flutist, called me up and said would I write him a flute concerto?

00:45:34.370 --> 00:45:39.030
And I said no, because I don't like the idea of concertos, I don't like the idea of the

00:45:39.030 --> 00:45:44.250
soloist pitted against the orchestra, and I also don't sort of feel that way in the

00:45:44.250 --> 00:45:51.680
musical sociology of things, I prefer the ensemble rather than the soloist.

00:45:51.680 --> 00:45:55.330
But Ransom seemed to be sincerely interested in playing my music, and I scratched my head

00:45:55.330 --> 00:46:00.000
and thought well gee, no sense sending away a great first rate player who wants to play

00:46:00.030 --> 00:46:03.130
something we could come up with. So I thought for a bit, and I called him back and said,

00:46:03.190 --> 00:46:07.620
look, I really don't write concertos and explain why when I've just told you, but would you

00:46:07.620 --> 00:46:11.900
consider playing against multiple flutes, all of which you are playing pre-recorded on tape?

00:46:12.160 --> 00:46:15.080
And then he paused for a moment and said, that sounds very interesting. I'd like to try it.

00:46:22.100 --> 00:46:30.000
The Vermont Counterpoint and the Vermont Counterpoint are performed in

00:46:30.000 --> 00:46:35.020
two ways but one way many more times than the other the normal way is you're a flutist and you

00:46:35.020 --> 00:46:39.860
have a little cassette tape and the cassette is played and you play into a microphone against it

00:46:39.860 --> 00:46:49.540
in a hole of whatever size what's on that tape is a pre-recording of yourself playing flute alto

00:46:49.540 --> 00:46:55.510
flute and piccolo in 10 different separate parts on which you are the 11th

00:47:02.040 --> 00:47:15.440
and you can imagine trying to put down eleven perfect tracks of a piece that's ten minutes

00:47:15.440 --> 00:47:20.410
long. If I made one mistake on a track I would have to go all the way back to the beginning

00:47:20.410 --> 00:47:27.390
and fix it. So the tension began to mount in the studio, to say the least, and Steve

00:47:27.390 --> 00:47:30.000
was up in the control room and I was trying to just maintain my...

00:47:30.030 --> 00:47:34.320
my sanity and the quality of my playing on stage and we finally we finished a

00:47:34.320 --> 00:47:40.030
very difficult section which from very difficult high notes on the flute and

00:47:40.030 --> 00:47:44.690
everyone seemed satisfied we got back to the hotel it was 11 midnight after

00:47:44.690 --> 00:47:48.030
midnight I got a telephone call from Steve he said Ransom we have to

00:47:48.030 --> 00:47:50.590
completely redo the last section I hate it on flute I don't want to have it on

00:47:50.590 --> 00:47:57.470
piccolo instead I thought I thought I was going to jump through the phone and

00:47:57.470 --> 00:47:59.920
strangle the man but

00:48:00.000 --> 00:48:03.750
actually we redid it and he was absolutely right

